inithooks (2.3.6+keel8) trixie; urgency=high

  * 29tagid: the first boot page carries no third party script. It loaded two
    from ajax.turnkeylinux.org, naming the build, the version and the
    appliance in the URL path, and a jQuery from ajax.googleapis.com that
    nothing on the page used. Both ran in the operator's browser on a machine
    nobody had logged into yet, and neither could be attested by keel verify,
    which can account for every byte in the layer and nothing fetched at view
    time. The turnkeylinux endpoints answer 404 today, so what shipped was a
    beacon to a service that no longer exists.
  * tagid.sh: drop tagid_render_scripts, tagid_is_tagged, tagid_version and
    tagid_build, which existed only to build those URLs.
  * 29tagid: the fence pages in /var are rebuilt from the packaged ones on
    every run, not edited, so pages inherited from an older image lose
    whatever they loaded from another host, however it was written. The
    new copy is built beside the served directory and swapped in; if that
    fails the /var copy is removed and the fence serves the packaged pages.
    It runs under turnkey-init too, which is how a machine whose first boot
    did not complete, or one initialised before this version, gets its
    pages replaced; until then such a machine keeps the old page, which is
    served only while the fence is up.
  * 29tagid: no longer reads /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/01turnkey. The build tag it
    parsed out of the apt User-Agent had no other consumer, and common
    removes that file (Keel-Linux/common#6).

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Tue, 29 Sep 2026 04:39:56 +0000

inithooks (2.3.6+keel7) trixie; urgency=high

  * lib/init-fence.sh: the fence no longer reports a fence it did not put up.
    A family whose nat table cannot be used, which is the normal case in an
    LXC container whose host has not loaded (ip6)table_nat, had its REDIRECT
    rule skipped with a warning, and the service then started and reported
    success with the application ports reachable over that family. The port
    is now refused in the filter table instead, inserted ahead of the rules
    the appliance firewall accepts, so what the fence promises holds by
    another means: the fence page stays reachable on its own ports, as it
    became in 2.3.6+keel1, and the application behind the fenced port does
    not answer. The refusal is a TCP reset, because an IPv6 client given
    the default ICMP error waits for its timeout instead of failing, and it
    does not apply to loopback, which a REDIRECT never did either.
  * lib/init-fence.sh: a port that can be neither redirected nor refused is
    fatal, with a message, because then nothing is fencing it. Fatal means
    the unit fails and its stop-post removes every rule the fence
    installed, so the application answers on every port again; it does not
    mean the port is left closed.
  * lib/init-fence.sh: stopping removes the refusals with the redirects, so
    a stopped or failed fence leaves no port closed behind it.
  * turnkey-init-fence.service: ordered after netfilter-persistent and
    Webmin's webmin-iptables and webmin-ip6tables, which restore a saved
    firewall at boot. A restore flushes the tables it names and every fence
    rule in them, redirects and refusals alike; ordering covers boot, and a
    restore while the fence is up still unfences every port.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Tue, 29 Sep 2026 04:34:01 +0000

inithooks (2.3.6+keel6) trixie; urgency=high

  * keel-host-keys.service: generate the SSH host keys, the TLS pair in
    /etc/ssl/private and, where ssl-cert is installed, the snakeoil pair,
    before sshd, Webmin, the web servers, PostgreSQL, postfix and the init
    fence start. A layer no longer ships any private key (keel-core#8), so
    the machine makes its own on every boot where one is missing.
  * keel-host-keys: replace a key in use whose public half is one that was
    published inside a Keel layer or template, listed in
    /usr/lib/inithooks/shared-host-keys. A machine made from an image
    published before this fix, whose first boot hooks did not run, stops
    serving a key anyone can download. The replacement is logged at warning
    level to the auth facility, and a key that cannot be made fails the unit;
    the unit has no Condition, so a read-only /etc fails instead of being
    skipped. Everything it writes is created under umask 077.
  * 15regen-sslcert: restart Webmin with the web servers. It reads
    /etc/ssl/private/cert.pem and kept serving the previous key until the
    next reboot.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Tue, 29 Sep 2026 04:08:58 +0000

inithooks (2.3.6+keel5) trixie; urgency=high

  * declarative.py: log a deprecated field name at level "warn". It passed
    "warning", which the inithooks logger refuses, and log() caught only
    OSError, so InitLogError killed 00declarative on every boot where both
    candidate description paths exist and no description was rendered at all.
  * declarative.py: a log line can no longer stop the hook; a level the logger
    refuses goes to stderr like a log file that cannot be written.

